 "reading": "All three sides reach 15 methods. The scan refuses the changed operation itself, by name, under gaps strict: before's String.getBytes(String) and after's String.getBytes(Charset) are 'an array result of a JDK instance method', and after's read of StandardCharsets.UTF_8 is 'a static field of the JDK that is not a compile-time constant' (the mutant's StandardCharsets.US_ASCII the same). The unchanged callee hashpw(byte[],String) adds the refused Arrays.copyOf(byte[],int) (the NUL terminator), and two gaps with no approximation, StringBuilder.charAt in decode_base64 and Integer.parseInt of the cost; the other six gaps (StringBuilder and String.substring) have approximations, which strict mode doesn't accept as proof either. Before alone has one more gap, AssertionError.<init>, the unreachable wrapper the patch removes. So the preservation attempt is predicted to be refused at the conversion on both sides, before the cipher: the password-bytes family's shared demand (the UTF-16 to UTF-8 conversion, CVE-2025-22228's too) is exactly where the checker stops. Central's spring-security-crypto-5.2.0.RELEASE.jar gives after's gaps and refusals, at lines one lower (the removed import). The scan lists no BCrypt static initializer gap: <clinit> fills P_orig, S_orig and the base64 tables from constants.",
